1
00:00:01,399 --> 00:00:05,174
If you've played 7 Wonders Duel, you already know most of this game.

2
00:00:05,293 --> 00:00:13,718
You still draft from a pyramid of overlapping cards. You still play three rounds. And you can still win suddenly, before the last card is gone.

3
00:00:13,839 --> 00:00:22,861
But in 2024, designers Antoine Bauza and Bruno Cathala took that engine to Middle-earth, and changed what you're actually fighting over.

4
00:00:23,310 --> 00:00:30,487
This is The Lord of the Rings: Duel for Middle-earth. And to understand it, the best place to start is with what it took away.

5
00:00:31,288 --> 00:00:34,624
In 7 Wonders Duel, most games end at a scoreboard.

6
00:00:35,173 --> 00:00:40,495
You add up your blue cards, your wonders, your guilds and your coins, and the highest total wins.

7
00:00:40,615 --> 00:00:43,579
Duel for Middle-earth has no victory points at all.

8
00:00:44,029 --> 00:00:50,330
Every way to win is a race. There are three of them, and each one ends the game the instant someone gets there.

9
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:55,676
That one change ripples through everything else. So let's build the game up, piece by piece.

10
00:00:56,475 --> 00:00:58,823
First, the players aren't mirror images.

11
00:00:59,372 --> 00:01:02,368
One of you plays the Fellowship. The other plays Sauron.

12
00:01:02,488 --> 00:01:08,897
Sauron takes the first turn. To balance that, the Fellowship starts with three coins, and Sauron with two.

13
00:01:09,016 --> 00:01:15,507
Both sides draft from the same cards. The difference is in what the Ring does for each of you, and we'll get to that.

14
00:01:16,307 --> 00:01:17,996
The turn will feel familiar.

15
00:01:18,546 --> 00:01:24,635
Each chapter, the cards are laid out in a structure, and you can only take a card that nothing else is covering.

16
00:01:25,084 --> 00:01:27,249
On your turn, you have three options.

17
00:01:27,368 --> 00:01:30,024
You can take a card and play it, paying its cost.

18
00:01:30,144 --> 00:01:37,192
You can take a card and discard it for coins: one coin in chapter one, two in chapter two, and three in chapter three.

19
00:01:37,313 --> 00:01:46,921
Or you can leave the cards alone and take a Landmark instead. That's this game's version of a wonder, and it's one of the biggest changes. So, hold that thought.

20
00:01:47,721 --> 00:01:48,881
Now, the cost.

21
00:01:49,430 --> 00:01:55,412
In 7 Wonders Duel, cards cost resources: wood, clay, stone, glass and papyrus.

22
00:01:55,533 --> 00:02:01,237
Here, the brown and grey cards collapse into a single grey colour, and they give you skills instead.

23
00:02:01,358 --> 00:02:05,355
There are five: Ruse, Strength, Courage, Knowledge and Leadership.

24
00:02:05,805 --> 00:02:10,976
And here's the part that will change your habits. If you're missing a skill, you pay one coin for it.

25
00:02:11,097 --> 00:02:21,302
In 7 Wonders Duel, a missing resource cost two coins, plus one more for every copy your opponent produced. That's what made hate-drafting brown cards so strong.

26
00:02:21,423 --> 00:02:26,604
Here the price is flat. One coin per missing skill, no matter what your opponent has built.

27
00:02:26,724 --> 00:02:35,746
So grey cards are about making your own turns cheaper, not about squeezing the other player. And coins are simple: yellow cards hand them to you straight away.

28
00:02:36,546 --> 00:02:39,338
Here's the full colour map, next to the game you know.

29
00:02:39,889 --> 00:02:42,212
Grey is skills. Yellow is coins.

30
00:02:42,332 --> 00:02:46,542
Blue cards no longer give points. They move you along the Quest of the Ring.

31
00:02:46,663 --> 00:02:51,062
Green still means collecting symbols, but now they're the races of Middle-earth.

32
00:02:51,182 --> 00:02:55,224
Red cards no longer push a conflict pawn. They put armies on a map.

33
00:02:55,344 --> 00:03:04,408
And purple cards, like the guilds, only show up in the final chapter. They let you move your units, make your opponent lose coins, or remove an enemy unit.

34
00:03:04,859 --> 00:03:12,760
Chaining is back, too. From chapter two on, if you already have a card with a matching chain symbol, you can play the new card for free.

35
00:03:13,561 --> 00:03:18,411
Now, the three ways to win. The first is the story itself: the Quest of the Ring.

36
00:03:18,961 --> 00:03:26,137
Above the cards runs a track toward Mount Doom. On it are two pieces: Frodo and Sam, and the Nazgûl chasing them.

37
00:03:26,257 --> 00:03:30,467
Blue cards carry Ring symbols, and each symbol moves your piece one space.

38
00:03:30,587 --> 00:03:37,284
When the Fellowship plays them, Frodo and Sam head for Mount Doom. If they get there, the Fellowship wins on the spot.

39
00:03:37,405 --> 00:03:45,113
When Sauron plays them, the Nazgûl move instead, closing in. If they catch Frodo and Sam, Sauron takes the Ring, and wins.

40
00:03:45,234 --> 00:03:52,942
And here's the clever part. The Nazgûl ride on top of the hobbits' piece. So whenever Frodo and Sam move, the Nazgûl come with them.

41
00:03:53,062 --> 00:04:00,921
The Fellowship can never pull away. The gap only ever shrinks. It's a race for one player, and a hunt for the other, on the same track.

42
00:04:01,042 --> 00:04:07,301
Some spaces along the way also hold a one-time bonus, which you take the moment you reach or pass it.

43
00:04:08,102 --> 00:04:11,522
The second way to win will feel like home to science players.

44
00:04:12,073 --> 00:04:16,007
In 7 Wonders Duel, six different science symbols win you the game.

45
00:04:16,127 --> 00:04:22,685
Here, green cards carry the symbols of six races: Elves, Dwarves, Hobbits, Humans, Ents and Wizards.

46
00:04:22,805 --> 00:04:27,079
Gather all six different races, and you win by the Support of the Races.

47
00:04:27,529 --> 00:04:33,757
Pairs still matter, too. In the original, a matching pair of science symbols earned you a progress token.

48
00:04:33,877 --> 00:04:42,110
Here, the moment you have two matching race symbols, you reveal the top two Alliance tokens of that race. You keep one, and put the other back.

49
00:04:42,230 --> 00:04:49,310
Most Alliance tokens give a lasting ability, so every pair is an engine piece, as well as a step toward victory.

50
00:04:49,430 --> 00:04:58,271
There's also a once-per-game bonus. When you first hold three different races, you reveal the top token of each of those three races, and keep one.

51
00:04:58,391 --> 00:05:04,894
And watch out for the Eagle. It appears on one Alliance token, and it counts as one of the six symbols you need.

52
00:05:05,694 --> 00:05:11,384
The third way to win replaces the military track with something much bigger: a map of Middle-earth.

53
00:05:11,935 --> 00:05:17,884
It has seven regions: Lindon, Arnor, Rhovanion, Enedwaith, Rohan, Gondor and Mordor.

54
00:05:18,004 --> 00:05:22,632
The Fellowship starts with two units in Arnor. Sauron starts with two in Mordor.

55
00:05:22,752 --> 00:05:28,435
When you play a red card, you pick one of the two regions on its banners, and place all of its units there.

56
00:05:28,555 --> 00:05:38,793
If enemy units are already there, a conflict breaks out. It's simple: each player removes one of their units, and you repeat until one side has none left in that region.

57
00:05:38,913 --> 00:05:44,896
So numbers matter. Arrive with three units against two, and you hold the region with one left standing.

58
00:05:45,016 --> 00:05:52,745
You're present in a region if you have at least one unit or a fortress there. Be present in all seven at once, and you've conquered Middle-earth.

59
00:05:52,865 --> 00:05:59,700
It's the military victory, stretched across a whole map. Instead of pushing one pawn, you're keeping seven plates spinning.

60
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:03,603
Which brings us back to Landmarks, this game's wonders.

61
00:06:04,153 --> 00:06:08,576
There are seven, one for each region, and three of them are face up at a time.

62
00:06:08,697 --> 00:06:17,485
Instead of taking a card, you can spend your turn on one. Each has a coin cost, plus one extra coin for every fortress you already have on the board.

63
00:06:17,605 --> 00:06:26,476
When you build it, you place a fortress in its region. Conflicts only remove units, so a fortress is a foothold your opponent can't win back by fighting.

64
00:06:26,596 --> 00:06:41,901
Every Landmark also has a one-time effect. Helm's Deep adds three units in Rohan. Minas Tirith adds a unit in Gondor and moves you two spaces on the quest. And Barad-dûr lets you search the discard pile and play a card from it for free.

65
00:06:42,021 --> 00:06:47,767
So a Landmark is a fortress, a bonus, and sometimes a step on another front, all in one turn.

66
00:06:48,567 --> 00:06:52,628
If nobody has won by the end of chapter three, there's still no scoring.

67
00:06:53,178 --> 00:06:58,649
Instead, you count regions. Whoever is present in the most regions of Middle-earth wins.

68
00:06:58,769 --> 00:07:00,976
And if you're tied, you share the victory.

69
00:07:01,776 --> 00:07:03,646
So what does all of this add up to?

70
00:07:04,197 --> 00:07:12,055
In 7 Wonders Duel, you could quietly build points for most of the game, and the sudden wins were threats you mostly defended against.

71
00:07:12,175 --> 00:07:20,387
Here, there are only sudden wins. Every card you take either pushes you toward one of three finish lines, or keeps your opponent away from theirs.

72
00:07:20,507 --> 00:07:30,967
And the fronts feed each other. Landmarks put fortresses on the map, and can push the quest. Quest spaces hand out bonuses. Races unlock alliances that bend the rules.

73
00:07:31,088 --> 00:07:36,983
So the real skill is reading three races at once, and knowing which one your opponent is about to win.

74
00:07:37,434 --> 00:07:41,911
Same pyramid of cards you know. A completely different game sitting on top of it.

